{"url": "https://www.ckwri.tamuk.edu/current-openings/population-dynamics-north-pacific-albatrosses", "final_url": "https://www.ckwri.tamuk.edu/current-openings/population-dynamics-north-pacific-albatrosses", "status": 200, "method": "http", "fetched": "2026-11-14", "text": "Population Dynamics of North Pacific Albatrosses | Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute\nSkip to main content\nSearch\nGlobal Menu\nEnews Sign up\nTeam Portal\nMenu\nMain navigation\nAbout\nCaesar Kleberg\nCaesar Kleberg Foundation\nCaesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute\nAdvisory Board\nDevelopment Team\nScience Team\nOperations Team\nGraduate Students\nProspective Students\nResearch Programs\nPatton Center For Deer Research\nFeline Research Program\nFire Ecology\nHabitat Management, Restoration & Research\nLandscape Ecology\nLivestock & Wildlife Relationships\nMolecular Genetics\nQuail Associates 2.0\nHenry Hamman Program for Hill Country Conservation and Management\nNorth Texas research Program\nRichard M. Kleberg Jr Center for Quail Research\nSouth Texas Natives\nTexas Native Seeds Program (TNS)\nWaterfowl and Wetland Birds\nWildlife Photography Program\nWildlife Diseases, Parasitology and Toxicology Research Program\nWildlife Center & Facilities\nAll Facilities\nTio and Janell Kleberg Wildlife Research Park\nCKWRI Ocelot Conservation Facility\nGiving\nMake a Donation\nCurrent Named Endowments\nNamed Giving Opportunities\nDevelopment Team\nGiving Forms\nPublications\nNews & Events\nMedia\nA Talk on the Wild Side Podcast\nSomewhere West of Wall Street\nWest of Texas\nEmployment\nContact\nBreadcrumb\nPopulation Dynamics of North Pacific Albatrosses\nPopulation Dynamics of North Pacific Albatrosses\nPosition Description\nWe are seeking a highly motivated Ph.D. student to pursue research that bridges seabird ecology, demographic modeling, and wildlife conservation. The successful candidate will leverage extensive long-term datasets to understand the intrinsic and extrinsic factors driving populations of two species of North Pacific albatrosses \u2013 Laysan Albatross (M\u014dl\u012b) and Black-footed Albatross (Ka\u2018upu). The research will primarily focus on breeding colonies at Midway Atoll, with potential expansion to include populations on other islands. This project has a strong quantitative focus. The core of the research involves the development of advanced demographic models, including Integrated Population Models, to understand how local and broad-scale environmental factors and fisheries affect population status, vital rates, and demographic trends. However, the student will have significant latitude to co-develop specific research questions and analytical approaches. The student may have the opportunity (contingent upon availability of funds) to participate in field deployments to the Pacific to assist with ongoing monitoring efforts and collect targeted field data in support of their specific project objectives. The successful applicant will be expected to publish their research in peer-reviewed scientific journals and present their findings at scientific conferences. This is a highly collaborative initiative, and the student will work closely with a diverse team of researchers, field biologists, and wildlife managers, including partners at the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service (USFWS) and the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS). The successful candidate will pursue a Ph.D. degree in Wildlife and Rangeland Sciences with the Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute and Department of Rangeland and Wildlife Sciences, Texas A&M University-Kingsville.\nRequired Qualifications\n\u2022 M.S. in Wildlife Science, Ecology, Statistics, or closely related field from an accredited university.\n\u2022 Strong quantitative and problem-solving skills, with experience in ecological modeling and statistical computing.\n\u2022 Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to engage with diverse audiences (scientists, wildlife managers, and the public).\n\u2022 Strong work ethic with the ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a research team.\nPREFERRED Qualifications\n\u2022 Demonstrated record of applied ecological research through peer-reviewed publications, presentations, or other scholarly activities.\n\u2022 Experience with demographic modeling within Bayesian frameworks (e.g., JAGS, Nimble).\n\u2022 Experience with geospatial technologies (e.g., GIS) and remote sensing data products (e.g., climate and oceanographic data).\n\u2022 Knowledge of seabird ecology or avian population dynamics\n\u2022 Experience conducting basic field ecology.\nStipend/Salary\n$2,500/mth plus benefits (medical package available after completion of waiting period). Nonresident tuition is waived (resident tuition and fees apply). Assistance of resident-tuition fees will be provided up to but not exceeding $12,000/year.\nStart Date\nJanuary 2027\nApplication Deadline\nWe will begin reviewing applications beginning 7 October 2026 and will continue until a suitable candidate is selected.\nTo Apply\nApplication packet must consist of: (1) cover letter summarizing your experience and interest in the position; (2) CV; (3) unofficial academic transcripts; (4) contact information for two professional references; and (5) one writing sample. Please send application materials (as a single PDF file) or address any questions regarding the position to Dr. Chris Malachowski (christopher.malachowski@tamuk.edu; Caesar Kleberg Wildlife Research Institute, Texas A&M University-Kingsville) using the email subject line of \u2018Albatross Demography Assistantship\u2019.\nTexas law requires that males 18 through 25 show proof of compliance with Federal Selective Service law in order to be eligible for employment. Selected candidate must pass a pre-employment background investigation to be hired for this position. EEO/AA/ADA\nDonate to\nCKWRI\nLearn more about how you can impact future of wildlife and wildlife hab